<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hwando</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wandu Mountain City</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hwando was a mountain fortress capital of the Korean kingdom of Goguryeo, near modern Ji'an in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jilin">Jilin province</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, paired with the walled city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gungnae">Gungnae</a> in the plain below. It was sacked in 244 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanqiu-jian">Guanqiu Jian</a> and in 342 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a>. Its ruins are part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> site of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capital-cities-and-tombs-of-the-ancient-koguryo-kingdom">Capital Cities and Tombs of the Ancient Koguryo Kingdom</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hwando.jpg" alt="Hwando" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Prcshaw · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Overlooking_noble_tombs.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
